WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.360
Star Wars has been around for decades, so
it's a sad but inevitable reality that many

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.440
of the actors who have appeared in that galaxy
far, far away are no longer with us.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.460
Here are a few faces from the franchise you
may not have realized passed away.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.020
Admiral Ackbar had a pretty small role in
the original Star Wars trilogy, in fact, he

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.140
only appeared briefly in Return of the Jedi.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.500
Even still, the character's appearance made
him a firm fan favorite, as did the iconic

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.160
line that has now passed into pop culture
legend.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:29.320
"It's a trap!"

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.080
"Wow, Admiral Ackbar!"

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.620
Naturally, then, fans of Star Wars mourned
the unfortunate passing of Ackbar's voice

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.560
actor, Erik Bauersfeld, who died in 2016 at
the age of 93.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.800
Interestingly, Bauersfeld once argued that
he thought of his movie roles as a secondary

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.560
career that he had accidentally stumbled into,
as opposed to his more solid work as a radio

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:50.060
producer.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:50.900
He once wrote:

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.140
"The voice work I did in movies was accidental.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.720
I was working with Randy Thom on radio dramas
at his technical quarters at Lucasfilm.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.960
One day Ben Burtt, sound designer for Star
Wars, came by and asked if I would audition

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.080
for a voice in the movie."

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.860
Bauersfeld also voiced Bib Fortuna, one of
Jabba the Hutt's associates, and he returned

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:11.930
to the role of Admiral Ackbar in The Force
Awakens, which released just months before

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.000
he passed away.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:18.500
As he dies in the opening hour of the film,
Uncle Owen can be considered a pretty minor

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.479
Star Wars character in the grand scheme of
things, but his death is the primary call

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.440
to arms that sets Luke Skywalker off on his
hero's journey.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:30.130
So in a weird way, Owen is actually one of
the movie's most important characters, making

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.850
actor Phil Brown one of the story's pivotal
players.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.680
Sadly, Brown passed away in 2006 at the age
of 89.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.020
Even though he was well-known when Star Wars
was made, he'd faced plenty of hardships as

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:41.520
an actor.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.780
In the 1950s, Brown was caught up in Senator
Joseph McCarthy's communist trials, and was

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:50.829
actually blacklisted because he refused to
comply with the House Un-American Activities

00:01:50.829 --> 00:01:53.380
Committee's demands to name communists in
Hollywood.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.580
He denied being a communist himself, but was
still forced to move to London to continue

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:58.080
acting.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.500
As it happened, George Lucas was filming in
London at the same time and needed a local

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.660
actor with an American accent, so he brought
Brown on board.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.780
Not long after, Brown was with Lucas in Tunisia,
filming A New Hope's early scenes on Tatooine.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:17.080
A powerhouse English actress, Shelagh Fraser
died in 2000 at the age of 79.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.640
She had played a small but memorable role
in A New Hope, as Luke's aunt Beru.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.010
The character dies early on in the story,
so Fraser was unable to contribute a lot to

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:30.400
the film, but just like Lars, Beru's death
spurs Luke into action, driving his character

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.690
arc through much of the trilogy.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.200
And even though her role in Star Wars is small,
some would argue that she's indirectly responsible

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.940
for Luke becoming the hero he is.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:42.959
At a New York Comic-Con panel, author Meg
Cabot argued that very case, suggesting that

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:46.940
Aunt Beru's kindness and caring for Luke helped
him overcome the Dark Side and defeat the

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:47.440
Empire.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.160
It also helped him find the compassion to
look for the good left in Darth Vader, without

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.920
whom he never would have stopped Emperor Palpatine.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.960
So yeah, nice work, Aunt Beru.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.900
Drewe Henley appeared in the Star Wars franchise
as Red Leader, the pilot who led Luke's squadron

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:04.580
in the strike against the Death Star at the
climax of A New Hope.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:09.080
Sadly, he was accidentally credited as "Drewe
Hemley" at the end of the film, but hey, he

00:03:09.091 --> 00:03:13.010
at least managed to squeeze in a post-mortem
appearance in Rogue One, thanks to the power

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.180
of archive footage.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.560
In the first film, Red Leader actually nearly
destroys the Death Star.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.240
He launches his torpedoes at the exhaust port,
but his aim is slightly off.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.360
Not long after his miss, his ship is destroyed,
and the poor guy is subsequently killed.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.230
Henley was a well-respected actor when he
was cast in A New Hope, but was forced to

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:33.500
retire not long after the film was released
due to his struggles with bipolar disorder.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:37.400
After working through his mental illness,
he settled down to run a bed and breakfast

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.040
with his wife.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.500
Henley died in 2016 at the age of 75.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.180
Anakin Skywalker has been played by multiple
actors across the Star Wars franchise, but

00:03:47.189 --> 00:03:52.049
when his deformed, weary face is revealed
in Star Wars: Return of the Jedi, that's classically

00:03:52.049 --> 00:03:54.220
trained actor Sebastian Shaw playing the role.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.360
He also portrayed Anakin's Force ghost at
the end of the original version of Return

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.380
of the Jedi, before being replaced by Hayden
Christensen in subsequent releases.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:06.000
Shaw was mostly known as a stage actor outside
of Star Wars fandom.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:10.600
He was a member of the Royal Shakespeare Company,
where he often played lead or featured roles.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:15.209
He was also a Broadway veteran, and was well
known for taking on parts in daring and controversial

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:15.780
plays.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:20.320
Lucas choosing to digitally replace Shaw with
Christensen in Return of the Jedi sparked

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.510
some controversy at the time, but Star Wars
fans will still never forget the reveal of

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:25.840
Darth Vader's face.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.190
Shaw passed away in 1994 at the age of 89.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:34.700
Jason Wingreen didn't actually appear in the
Star Wars films in person, but his voice brought

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.080
one of the saga's most iconic villains to
life.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:41.680
Wingreen was the original voice of Boba Fett,
the intergalactic bounty hunter who tracks

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.040
down Han Solo during The Empire Strikes Back.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:48.440
Wingreen was a well-known television actor
when he auditioned for Star Wars, having appeared

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:51.700
in a number of different shows throughout
the '60s, '70s and '80s.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:56.190
He originally auditioned for the role of Yoda,
but lost out on the role and was cast as Boba

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:59.640
Fett instead, and only recorded four lines
for the final film.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.940
He's not even credited, and it wasn't until
the year 2000 that the public discovered who

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:05.140
had voiced the villain.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:09.390
Still, it's a testament to Wingreen's talent
that Boba Fett became such a massively popular

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:10.830
character nonetheless.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.510
Wingreen passed away in 2016 at the age of
95.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.810
You may never have seen Kenny Baker's face
or heard his voice in a Star Wars film, but

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:24.350
it's a fair bet you still know him, for most
of the movies in the franchise, he portrayed

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.780
one of sci-fi's most iconic characters: R2-D2.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:33.180
R2-D2 wasn't a puppet or an animatronic like
so many other creatures in the Star Wars universe.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.480
Instead, there was an actor inside that droid.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.280
Baker was only 3 foot 8 inches, so he could
fit inside the chassis used for filming, and

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:43.600
he's the one responsible for crafting much
of R2-D2's "personality."

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:48.340
When Baker passed away in 2016 at the age
of 81, StarWars.com wrote:

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:53.480
"Whether it was the slow turn of Artoo's dome
to convey suspicion or nervous wobbles signifying

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:56.900
fear, Baker made a robotic being seem very
human."

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:01.090
While Baker played the droid in all three
original films and two prequels, he also played

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:02.810
an Ewok in Return of the Jedi.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:08.240
Peter Cushing was a massive star when he was
cast as Grand Moff Tarkin in A New Hope.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:13.020
He also brought an immediate gravitas to the
film, oozing menace and calm and somehow coming

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.680
off every bit as intimidating as Darth Vader
himself.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.420
"Charming, to the last."

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.640
Before appearing in A New Hope, Cushing was
an icon in horror cinema.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:28.550
He appeared in many of the Dracula and Frankenstein
films of the '50s and '60s, and also portrayed

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:30.740
Sherlock Holmes in a few movie adaptations.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:36.160
After a long and illustrious career, Cushing
passed away in 1994 at the age of 81.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.100
Despite this, however, he technically reprised
his role as Tarkin in Rogue One: A Star Wars

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.060
Story, even though the film came out over
20 years after his death.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.620
Cushing was recreated in the film with state
of the art CGI, nearly climbing out of the

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:50.340
uncanny valley in the process.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:51.040
Nearly.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:56.600
Even though Obi-Wan Kenobi died in A New Hope,
Alec Guiness appeared in the other films of

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.580
the original trilogy, thanks to the power
of the Force.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:04.400
His casting lent some credibility to the original
film, but he also infamously viewed the story

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:06.740
as, in his words, "fairy tale rubbish."

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:11.060
Guinness was a hugely famous actor when he
signed on for George Lucas' space opera.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.560
He'd won an Oscar for The Bridge on the River
Kwai and had starred in classics such as Lawrence

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:17.200
of Arabia and Doctor Zhivago.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:21.420
And while many of the leading actors in Star
Wars are big names now, they weren't at the

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:25.200
time, making Guinness by far the biggest star
in the movie in 1977.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.300
But why did he join the project if he hated
the material?

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:29.980
Well, there were a couple of reasons.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:33.220
First, he thought the moral of the story was
admirable enough.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.930
Second, he wouldn't have to do any publicity
for the film.

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.960
And third, the studio offered him a massive
salary.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.140
Of course, he ignored any fan mail he received
about Star Wars, and hated discussing his

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:44.180
role as Obi-Wan.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:48.640
Sir Alec Guinness passed away in the year
2000 at the age of 86.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:54.020
One of the most prolific actors in the Star
Wars franchise, Christopher Lee's career spans

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:56.840
over 250 roles over the length of his career.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:01.140
Lee is absolutely one of the gods of geekdom,
and has graced several of Hollywood's most

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:02.280
notable franchises.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:07.360
Here, he portrayed Count Dooku in two of the
prequel films, Attack of the Clones and Revenge

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:07.880
of the Sith.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:12.820
Lee became a star due to his many appearances
in the Hammer Horror Dracula franchise, where,

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:17.360
funnily enough, he frequently collaborated
with another Star Wars alum: Peter Cushing.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:21.840
Lee's imposing figure and commanding voice
made him perfectly suited to playing the villain,

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:26.460
which helped him a great deal in arguably
his biggest ever film role: playing Saruman

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:27.640
in The Lord of the Rings.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:32.000
A man of many talents, Christopher Lee also
released a number of heavy metal albums when

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:33.580
he was in his 80s and 90s.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:38.360
He was also knighted, served in the Second
World War as a member of the RAF and was attached

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:42.860
to the British Army's Long Range Desert Group,
the precursor of the modern-day SAS.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:46.360
He passed away in 2015 at the age of 93.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:51.890
Despite never having showed his face on-screen
in Star Wars, Peter Mayhew quickly became

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:54.040
one of the saga's most iconic characters.

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:58.310
His performance as Chewbacca helped bring
a sense of real humanity to a character where

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:01.240
many other actors could easily have been lost
beneath the mask.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:06.480
"Laugh it up, fuzzball."

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:11.680
Mayhew was a hospital orderly when he auditioned
for Chewbacca, and even kept that job until

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:13.720
after Return of the Jedi was released.

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:18.040
The 7 foot 3 inch actor then essentially made
his living off the role for the rest of his

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:21.450
life, attending conventions as a fan favorite
star for decades.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:25.260
He eventually reprised the role in 2015's
The Force Awakens.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.460
Nearly everyone who worked with Mayhew on
the Star Wars franchise had nothing but kind

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:30.540
things to say about him.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:34.500
After his death in April 2019, Mark Hamill
wrote on Twitter:

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.580
"He was the gentlest of giants.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:41.080
A big man with an even bigger heart who never
failed to make me smile, and a loyal friend

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:42.160
who I loved dearly.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:46.800
I'm grateful for the memories we shared and
I'm a better man for just having known him."

